.TH CC 1
.SH NAME
cc \- C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cc
[ option ] ... file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cc\^
is the 
.SM UNIX 
C compiler.
.PP
.I Cc\^
accepts several types of arguments:
.PP
Arguments whose names end with `.c' are taken to be
C source programs; they are compiled, and
each object program is left on the file
whose name is that of the source with `.o' substituted
for `.c'.
The `.o' file is normally deleted if a single
C program is compiled and loaded.
.PP
In the same way,
arguments whose names end with `.s' are taken to be assembly source programs
and are assembled, producing a `.o' file.
.PP
The following options are interpreted by
.IR cc .
See
.IR ld\^ (1)
for link editor options.
.TP \w'\fB\-o\ \fIoutput\fR\ \ 'u
.B \-c
Suppress the link edit phase of the compilation, and force
an object file to be produced even if only one program is compiled.
.TP
.B \-n
Passed on to
.I ld\^
to make the text of the resulting program shared.
.TP
.B \-p
Arrange for the compiler to produce code
which counts the number of times each routine is called;
also, if link editing takes place, replace the standard startup
routine by one which automatically calls
.IR monitor\^ (3C)
at the start and arranges to write out a
.I mon.out
file at normal termination of execution of the object program.
An execution profile can then be generated by
use of
.IR prof\^ (1).
.TP
.B \-fsky
Use the sky floating point library.
.TP
.B \-O(BKPS)
Invoke an
object-code improver (optimizer).  If
.B B 
is specified,
``jump to subroutine'' instructions are changed to
``branch to subroutine'' instructions (where possible).
If 
.B K
is specified, certain 
.SM UNIX
kernel optimizer functions are not performed.
If
.B P
is specified, stack probe instructions are removed.
(Note:  
.B P
should only be used for the operating system source.)
If 
.B S
is specified, stack frame optimization is performed and the debugger,
.IR adb\^ (1),
might indicate too few subroutine parameters on stack trace back.
.TP
.B \-R (addr)
Pass on to
.IR ld ,
making the resulting object module 
.IR origin ed 
at
.IR addr(hex) .
.TP
.B \-S
Compile the named C programs, and leave the
assembler-language output on corresponding files suffixed `.s'.
.TP
.B \-E
Run only 
.IR cpp\^ (1)
on the named C programs, and send the result to
standard output.
.TP
.B \-P
Run only the macro preprocessor
on the named C programs, and send the result to the
corresponding files suffixed. `.i'
.TP
.B \-C
Prevent the macro preprocessor from eliding (leaving out) comments.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Name the final executable output file
.IR output .
If this option is used the file "a.out" will be left undisturbed.
.TP
.BI \-D name=def
.br
.ns
.TP
.BI \-D \*Sname
Define the
.I name\^
to the preprocessor,
as if by
.BR #define .
If no definition is given, the name is defined as "1".
.TP
.BI \-U \*Sname
Remove any initial definition of
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-I \*Sdir
.B #include
files whose names do not begin with `/' are always
sought first in the directory 
of the
.I file\^
argument,
then in directories named in 
.B \-I
options,
then in the directory 
.BR /usr/include .
.TP
.B \-v
print the name of each subprocess as it is executing.
.PP
Other arguments
are taken
to be either link editor option arguments, or C-compatible
object programs, typically produced by an earlier
.I cc\^
run,
or perhaps libraries of C-compatible routines.
These programs, together with the results of any
compilations specified, are linked via 
.IR ld\^ (1) 
(in the order
given) to produce an executable program with name
.IR a.out .
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
cc \-o output prog1.c prog2.c prog3.c
.PP
would compile code in the three named C programs and put the
compiled code into the file "output".
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/usr/c/occom\ \ \ \ \ 'u
file.c	input file
.br
file.o	object file
.br
a.out	linked output
.br
/tmp/ctm?	temporary
.br
/lib/cpp	preprocessor
.br
/lib/c	combined compiler pass1 and pass2
.br
/lib/c0		compiler pass1
.br
/lib/c1		compiler pass2
.br
/lib/c2		optional optimizer invoked with "-O"
.br
/lib/crt0.o	runtime startoff
.br
/lib/mcrt0.o	runtime startoff for profiling
.br
/lib/libc.a	standard library, see section 3
.br
/usr/include	standard directory for '#include' files
.br
/lib/libm.a	math library
.br
/lib/libsky.a	sky floating point routines
.br
/lib/crt0sky.o	runtime startoff using sky
.br
/lib/mcrt0sky.o	runtime startoff for profiling using sky
.DT
.SH "SEE ALSO"
adb(1), ld(1), lint(1), prof(1), monitor(3C) 
.br
